# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Contextual Governance — every variable the finished system needs.
#
# Most services do not read these yet: this slice is stubs plus a deploy
# pipeline. The list is complete now so nobody discovers a missing secret
# during integration. Copy to `.env` and fill in.
#
# On Render, the ones marked SECRET are set in the dashboard (sync: false in
# render.yaml, so a blueprint sync prompts for them rather than committing
# them). Never commit a filled-in `.env` — it is gitignored.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# --- Model ------------------------------------------------------------------
# SECRET. console.anthropic.com → Settings → API keys.
#
# Read by cg-web only (#14): the Mastra agent behind POST /api/chat. Without it
# that route answers 503 naming this variable, GET /health reports
# `"agent": "missing"` and `"status": "degraded"`, and the home page's
# configuration banner says so — a deployment that can sign Dana in and then
# cannot run a turn is not fine.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# Model id kept in env so it can be swapped without a code change.
MODEL_ID=claude-sonnet-5

# --- Arcade -----------------------------------------------------------------
# SECRET. api.arcade.dev dashboard → API keys.
ARCADE_API_KEY=
# Where apps/web executes tools. Unset means Arcade itself, which is what a
# deploy wants.
#
# **Offline mode.** Point this at the local stand-in and the two beats of the
# approval flow run with no Arcade account and no network:
#
#   bun run --cwd apps/web arcade-stand-in     # prints the port it bound
#   ARCADE_API_URL=http://localhost:<that port> ARCADE_API_KEY=offline \
#     PORT=3000 bun run --cwd apps/web dev
#
# The stand-in is a development fixture, not the product: it calls the real
# /pre on apps/hooks and runs the Decide tool only when the answer is OK, so
# the refusal you see is the actual policy refusing — but the transport is
# ours. Full three-terminal walkthrough in apps/web/README.md. ARCADE_API_KEY
# must be non-empty even offline; the stand-in ignores the value.
# ARCADE_API_URL=
# The MCP gateway slug you create in the Arcade dashboard. Mastra's MCPClient
# connects to https://api.arcade.dev/mcp/${ARCADE_GATEWAY_ID}.
ARCADE_GATEWAY_ID=
# SECRET. Shared secret Arcade signs hook requests with, so `apps/hooks` can
# reject anything that did not come from the engine. Arcade dashboard →
# Contextual access → your hook → signing secret.
ARCADE_HOOK_SIGNING_SECRET=
# Toolkit names, as Arcade files the deployed toolkits. `arcade deploy` reads
# the MCPApp's name off its `initialize` response and PascalCases it: the
# server named `loan` is toolkit `Loan`, and a probe named `loan_mcp_probe`
# came back `LoanMcpProbe` (so underscores are consumed and, unlike Remote MCP
# registration, `mcp` is not stripped). Tool names are PascalCased too, by
# arcade-mcp itself: `get_loan` is `Loan.GetLoan`. Measured on #35 off the
# workers API and by executing the fully-qualified names; see
# tools/loan/README.md. A rule keyed on the wrong string matches nothing,
# which is indistinguishable from a rule that permits.
#
# Two services read this and they use it for opposite things. On cg-hooks it
# keys the seeded policy rules, where a wrong value matches nothing. On cg-web
# (#14) it is the ALLOW-LIST deciding which of the gateway's tools the agent is
# handed: a live tools/list for a signed-in persona returns eight entries — the
# project's six plus the gateway's own System_ManageAuthorization and
# Arcade_ListApps — and only the ones whose wire names start with `Loan_` and
# `Approvals_` go to the model. A wrong value there selects nothing, and
# /api/chat answers 502 naming this variable rather than letting a model with
# no tools answer from memory about a loan book it never read.
ARCADE_LOAN_TOOLKIT=Loan
# Same rule applied to the `approvals` package, and measured on #35 once #18
# deployed it: the server named `approvals` is toolkit `Approvals`, with
# `Approvals.RequestApproval` and `Approvals.Decide` at 1.0.0. Derived first,
# then observed, and the two agreed.
ARCADE_APPROVALS_TOOLKIT=Approvals
# The Arcade auth provider id `apps/idp` is registered under (#13). Baked into
# every loan tool's auth requirement — see tools/loan/loan/__init__.py.
ARCADE_IDP_PROVIDER_ID=cg-idp

# Act 4's control run (#17). Read by `apps/hooks`.
#
# UNSET IS ARMED, and that is the point: the `/post` free-text scanners run
# unless somebody deliberately turned them off, so a rehearsal that forgot this
# line still has the protection. Accepted spellings are on/armed/true and
# off/disarmed/false; anything else is refused at boot rather than guessed at,
# because reading a typo as "armed" protects a demo that meant to show the
# control run and reading it as "disarmed" strips a demo that meant to have it.
#
#   INJECTION_DETECTION=off
#
# compiles the output policy WITHOUT the injection patterns, so the instruction
# planted in LN-2291's underwriter_notes reaches the model and the audience sees
# what was being prevented. Act 3's field redaction is untouched, so the
# contrast is about act 4 and nothing else. It needs a restart, which is why the
# on-stage flip is the policy database instead:
#
#   sqlite3 governance.db "UPDATE output_rules SET enabled = 0 \
#     WHERE id = 'post.strip-injected-instructions'"
#
# live within one POLICY_POLL_MS, and `= 1` puts it back. Either way `/health`
# reports `injection_detection.state: disarmed` with a warning, and every policy
# reload logs it: a control that is off must never be quiet about it.
INJECTION_DETECTION=

# --- Service addresses ------------------------------------------------------
# Addresses of the deployed services. Arcade Cloud calls the hooks over the
# public internet, so these must be reachable — no tunnels in the live path.
#
# These are HOST-form (`host` or `host:port`), NOT full URLs. Consumers add the
# scheme — http for localhost, https otherwise.
#
# On Render all three are `sync: false`, and the value is READ OFF THE RENDER
# SERVICE PAGE — never derived, never guessed. Guessing fails because
# onrender.com subdomains are global, so a taken service name gets silently
# suffixed: `cg-web` is `cg-web-sa31` and `cg-idp` is `cg-idp-or5b` in the live
# environment, and a hardcoded `cg-web.onrender.com` would address somebody
# else's service. Deriving fails because `fromService … property: host` emits
# the bare service name rather than the hostname — measured 2026-09-10 on #59,
# where `IDP_PUBLIC_HOST` arrived as `cg-idp-or5b` and every userinfo call went
# to `https://cg-idp-or5b/…`, which resolves nowhere. Blueprints have no string
# interpolation, so nothing in render.yaml can bridge the two.
#
# So: open the service's page in the Render dashboard, take the host part of the
# URL shown there, and paste it. Each consumer refuses anything it could not
# reach — a host with no dot that is not a loopback address, a port that is not
# a number, a full URL — at boot, naming the variable and pointing back at the
# service page.
HOOKS_PUBLIC_HOST=localhost:8081
# Read by `tools/loan` — where it is delivered as an Arcade secret, uploaded by
# `arcade deploy` from this file, because that is the one configuration channel
# a deployed toolkit has. `apps/hooks` is set from the same value and checks it
# at boot; #16's redaction work is its first real consumer.
LOAN_APP_PUBLIC_HOST=localhost:8082
# The identity provider (`apps/idp`, #36). `apps/loan-app` validates every
# bearer token against its /oauth2/userinfo and records the email it returns
# as the actor. Nothing else in the request names who is acting.
#
# Locally this is also the port `bun run dev:idp-stub` binds, so the stub and
# the API cannot disagree about where the identity provider is. See #56.
IDP_PUBLIC_HOST=localhost:8083

# The web service's own address, used to build approval links. Absent from
# render.yaml by design: Render injects RENDER_EXTERNAL_HOSTNAME into every
# service automatically, so on Render this variable is unset and the app falls
# back to that. Set it locally.
WEB_PUBLIC_HOST=localhost:3000

# There is deliberately no NEXT_PUBLIC_ variable anywhere in this file. The
# control-plane panel needs the hook host in the browser, but `next build`
# inlines NEXT_PUBLIC_* into the client bundle while Render supplies service
# env vars at runtime — so a NEXT_PUBLIC_HOOKS_HOST would be empty at build
# time and `undefined` in the browser, while working fine under `next dev`.
# The panel reads HOOKS_PUBLIC_HOST in a server component and passes it down
# as a prop instead (#21).

# Which stream the control-plane panel watches. One knob, three states, and the
# panel names the one it is in on screen:
#
#   hooks     the live control plane at HOOKS_PUBLIC_HOST above. Badge:
#             `LIVE · cg-hooks.onrender.com`
#   fixture   the built-in replay of #5's sequence. Badge: `FIXTURE REPLAY`
#   unset     the replay under `next dev`, so a fresh clone with no hook server
#             running still has something to look at — and NO STREAM AT ALL
#             when deployed (NODE_ENV=production, or Render's RENDER=true):
#             /panel renders an error state naming this variable, and /health
#             answers `"panel_stream":"unconfigured"` with `"status":"degraded"`
#
# Anything else is refused by name rather than resolved to something.
#
# The deployed half of that is #81, and it is not hypothetical. This key was
# missing from render.yaml between #21 and #81, an unset value quietly meant
# the replay, and on 2026-09-11 a human made a real governed Loan_GetLoan
# against the live gateway and watched the production panel play the demo
# sequence over the top of it, with nothing on the page saying so. A control
# surface showing something other than reality is the failure this project
# exists to argue against.
#
# `/panel?fixture=1` asks for the replay for one request, on any deployment.
GOVERNANCE_STREAM=

# --- Databases --------------------------------------------------------------
# bun:sqlite files, one owner each: governance.db belongs to apps/hooks,
# loans.db to apps/loan-app, idp.db to apps/idp. Never committed (*.db is
# gitignored).
#
# Seed-if-empty, not seed-on-boot: each owning service bootstraps from its
# checked-in fixture only when the database has no schema, and leaves it alone
# afterwards. So a loan approved on stage and a policy row edited on stage are
# both still there after a restart. Decided on #29 — the earlier model, where
# both paths sat in /tmp and redeploying was secretly the reset button, made a
# rule edited during act 1 vanish before act 3.
#
# Resetting is therefore an explicit script (#23), never a side effect of
# deploying. On Render each path points at that service's mounted disk; see
# `render.yaml`, including how to delete the disks and run ephemerally.
#
# Locally these are relative paths, and each service creates the parent
# directory if it is missing.
GOVERNANCE_DB_PATH=./governance.db
LOANS_DB_PATH=./loans.db
IDP_DB_PATH=./idp.db

# --- Identity provider (apps/idp) --------------------------------------------
# The enterprise's IdP, as a demo fixture. Better Auth, OAuth 2.1, own service.
#
# SECRET. Signs sessions and the OAuth query, and encrypts the ID-token
# signing key at rest in idp.db. Render generates it (generateValue: true).
# Locally, leave it unset and a fixed development secret is used. Rotating it
# logs everyone out and makes the stored signing key unreadable, so do not.
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=
# ⚠️ The OAuth client secret is stored HASHED and is printed exactly ONCE, by
# whichever run of `oauth-client` produced it. Write it down then. Before #70
# it was stored encrypted and could be re-printed on any later day; that is
# only permitted with Better Auth's JWT plugin off, which left this IdP with
# HS256 ID tokens and no jwks_uri — and an IdP with no published key set
# cannot back an Arcade User Source (#65, measured). If the secret is lost:
#
#   bun run --cwd apps/idp oauth-client --rotate
#
# which mints a new one under the SAME client id, so only the secret field in
# the Arcade dashboard has to change, not the whole registration.
# Public origin of the IdP, which is also the OAuth issuer. Absent from
# render.yaml by design: Render injects RENDER_EXTERNAL_URL into every service
# and the app falls back to it. Set it locally only if the port differs.
IDP_PUBLIC_URL=http://localhost:8083
# Comma-separated redirect URIs allowlisted on the first OAuth client. Arcade
# shows its own under the provider's "Redirect URL" in the dashboard; the code
# defaults to Arcade Cloud's. Changing this updates the client in place — the
# client id and secret do not change.
IDP_OAUTH_REDIRECT_URIS=https://cloud.arcade.dev/api/v1/oauth/callback
# Optional (#79). Comma-separated client KEYS, not credentials — Better Auth
# generates every client_id and client_secret and they cannot be pinned from
# env. Unset means exactly one client, `arcade`, which is what every deployment
# has had since #13; naming more gives each its own row, its own generated id,
# its own hashed secret and its own redirect allowlist. The reason to want a
# second one is that an Arcade User Source and an Arcade custom OAuth provider
# are two registrations with two generated redirect URIs, and a rotated secret
# then costs one dashboard field rather than two. The cost is that consent is
# recorded per client, so each persona consents once per client.
#
#   IDP_OAUTH_CLIENTS=arcade,arcade-user-source
#
# A key's own redirect allowlist is IDP_OAUTH_REDIRECT_URIS_<KEY>, upper-snake:
#
#   IDP_OAUTH_REDIRECT_URIS_ARCADE_USER_SOURCE=https://cloud.arcade.dev/oauth2/intermediate_callback
#
# Without it that client falls back to IDP_OAUTH_REDIRECT_URIS above. With more
# than one configured, `oauth-client --rotate` requires --client <key>.
IDP_OAUTH_CLIENTS=

# --- apps/web's identity (#82) -----------------------------------------------
# apps/web signs each persona in against apps/idp as its OWN OAuth client,
# "client C" — separate from the two registrations Arcade holds. DESIGN.md →
# Identity and OAuth. Then it drives the gateway OAuth itself (hop 1) and
# answers Arcade's custom user verifier (hop 2).
#
# Nothing here has a development fallback, and /health reports each of the three
# capabilities as `configured` or `missing`:
#
#   curl -s localhost:8080/health
#   {"status":"ok","service":"web","signin":"configured","gateway":"configured","verifier":"configured"}
#
# `status` is `degraded` — not `ok` — whenever any of the three is `missing`, and
# the home page carries a red banner naming the same problems. The response stays
# HTTP 200 either way, because Render abandons a deploy whose health check is not
# 200 and an instance that never comes up is one whose /health nobody can read.
#
# apps/idp's public origin, as a URL with a scheme — unlike the *_PUBLIC_HOST
# keys above, which are HOST-form. Same value as IDP_PUBLIC_URL when both run in
# one place.
IDP_ISSUER=http://localhost:8083
# Client C. Mint it by putting `web` in IDP_OAUTH_CLIENTS above and running, in
# apps/idp:
#
#   IDP_OAUTH_REDIRECT_URIS_WEB=http://localhost:3000/api/auth/callback \
#     bun run --cwd apps/idp oauth-client --client web --rotate
#
# That prints the secret exactly once — storage is hashed since #70. A lost
# secret costs one --rotate and one field here, never a re-registration.
IDP_CLIENT_ID=
IDP_CLIENT_SECRET=
# Seals the per-browser session cookie with AES-256-GCM. That cookie holds the
# persona's email and their gateway access and refresh tokens, so this is a real
# key and not a salt.
#
#   openssl rand -hex 32
#
# **At least 32 characters, and at least 8 distinct ones**, and that floor is
# enforced rather than advised: below it /health reports signin, gateway and
# verifier all `missing` and every identity route answers 503 naming the
# minimum. 32 because the derived key is 256 bits and SHA-256 does not add
# entropy — a shorter secret is the part an attacker actually has to guess. The
# distinct-character floor is there because length alone is satisfiable by
# padding, and `xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx` is thirty-two characters and
# about one bit.
#
# `SESSION_SECRET=x` was accepted until round 1 of #84's review: /health said
# `configured`, the built service reached Ready, and every sign-in worked, under
# a key anybody could guess. A refusal that only fires on an *absent* value
# misses the case a human actually produces.
#
# Deliberately no fallback in the code — a default key is a key everybody has.
# Rotating it signs every browser out, which is the whole blast radius.
SESSION_SECRET=
# apps/web's own public origin, with the scheme. Every OAuth redirect_uri is
# built from it (`${PUBLIC_URL}/api/auth/callback`, `/api/arcade/callback`) and
# each must be allowlisted byte for byte at the other end. It also decides
# whether the session cookie carries `Secure`: everything but an http://
# loopback value gets it, because a browser silently drops a Secure cookie sent
# over plain http and the symptom is a sign-in that works and then forgets.
PUBLIC_URL=http://localhost:3000
# What sign-in asks apps/idp for. `email` is not optional: the address is the
# join key between Arcade's user_id, the OAuth subject and the loan book's
# actor column, and a sign-in without it has no identity to seal.
IDP_SCOPES=openid email
# Where `confirm_user` lives, which is NOT api.arcade.dev. Measured on spike
# #75. The default is right; the variable exists so a test can point the
# verifier's two calls at a stand-in without moving the gateway's.
ARCADE_CLOUD_URL=https://cloud.arcade.dev
# Optional. Pins the MCP client id hop 1 authorizes under, instead of letting
# apps/web register one dynamically per process. Arcade renders its gateway
# consent screen once per persona per MCP client id, so pinning one means a
# redeploy does not cost every persona another consent click. Nothing in this
# repo requires it.
ARCADE_MCP_CLIENT_ID=
#
# One step here is not an environment variable: Arcade dashboard → Auth →
# Settings → Custom verifier route must be `${PUBLIC_URL}/api/arcade/verify`.
# Without it Arcade uses its own verifier, which demands an Arcade account that
# is a project member. Our personas are not, the grant binds to whoever happens
# to be signed in at account.arcade.dev, and the tool re-challenges forever with
# nothing on the panel to say why (DESIGN.md open risk 4).

# --- Personas ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Real email identities, so Arcade OAuth genuinely works. The persona switcher
# stands in for real login; the seam for real auth is documented in the README.
#
# apps/idp and apps/hooks read these too, when idp.db and governance.db are
# first seeded, so the IdP's accounts and the control plane's subjects carry
# the same addresses as the Arcade accounts — the email is the one join key
# between Arcade's user_id, the OAuth subject, the policy's subject and the
# loan book's actor. Left blank, both seed the placeholder addresses in
# apps/idp/src/fixtures/people.json (apps/hooks/src/fixtures/governance.json
# carries the same four). Set them before the first boot (or reset both
# databases after): a seed is not re-read.
#
# Case does not matter. Both services lowercase what they seed and the loan
# book lowercases the address the OAuth provider returns, so `Dana.Okafor@…`
# here and `dana.okafor@…` in Arcade are the same person. You do not have to
# match the Arcade account's capitalisation by hand.
PERSONA_DANA_EMAIL=
PERSONA_SAM_EMAIL=
PERSONA_RILEY_EMAIL=
PERSONA_MORGAN_EMAIL=

# --- Slack (approvals toolkit) ----------------------------------------------
# Nothing to set. Slack access is brokered by Arcade's stock Slack auth
# provider, so there is no bot token and no channel id here — the message is a
# DM to the routed approver, sent with the requester's own delegated token, and
# who was asked is decided by routing rather than configured.
#
# The toolkit declares four scopes, not three, exactly the set spike #3
# exercised: chat:write, im:write, users:read, users:read.email. users:read is
# a prerequisite for users:read.email — Slack refuses the authorize request
# outright without it — and im:write is what conversations.open needs. See
# docs/spikes/03-slack-scopes.md and tools/approvals/README.md.
#
# Every persona who can trigger act 2 authorizes Slack once. Rehearse it: a
# scope refusal happens upstream of every hook and leaves the panel dark.

# --- Control plane (apps/hooks) ----------------------------------------------
# Our own budget for answering a hook, in milliseconds, inside Arcade's 5s
# default. A request that runs past it is failed closed and audited as such.
# The measured cost of the largest /access call is tens of milliseconds
# (`bun run --cwd apps/hooks bench`), so this only matters when something is
# wrong — and then the point is that we decide, rather than Arcade timing us
# out and every tool in the project failing with "policy service could not be
# reached".
HOOK_DEADLINE_MS=2500
# How often, in milliseconds, the control plane checks governance.db for a
# live edit. The policy is served from memory and a hook call never reads it
# from disk; this poll of one integer is the only policy read. A row changed
# on stage is honoured within this interval, and /health shows
# policy.revision, policy.loaded_at and policy.last_poll_at.
POLICY_POLL_MS=250

# --- Approvals store (tools/approvals ↔ apps/hooks) --------------------------
# SECRET. Shared bearer the deployed approvals toolkit presents on the
# /approvals endpoints apps/hooks serves, so the record a human then acts on
# cannot be manufactured by anyone on the internet. Generate any long random
# string. Reaches the toolkit as an Arcade secret, uploaded by `arcade deploy`
# from this file — along with HOOKS_PUBLIC_HOST and WEB_PUBLIC_HOST above,
# which are the other two secrets tools/approvals declares.
#
# Three sides read this one variable: apps/hooks, which serves the four
# endpoints; tools/approvals, which writes and decides through them; and
# apps/web, whose approval page reads GET /approvals/{id}. See
# tools/approvals/README.md for the contract.
#
# Left blank, apps/hooks and apps/web both fall back to the same development
# token, so a local run needs no configuration. Under NODE_ENV=production
# neither will: apps/hooks refuses to boot and apps/web refuses to read its
# configuration, both with the same error, because the fallback is published in
# their source and a service authenticating with it in production would be
# doing so quietly. CI boots both images that way to prove the checks fire.
APPROVALS_STORE_TOKEN=

# How long a grant issued by an approval stays good, in seconds. A grant is
# single use *and* time-boxed; the expiry is the half that still holds when the
# retry never happens, so an approval nobody acted on stops being authority
# rather than sitting in the table forever. Long enough for the Slack round
# trip on stage, short enough that a stale approval cannot be replayed after
# the demo has moved on.
GRANT_TTL_SECONDS=900

# --- Reset (#106) -----------------------------------------------------------
# SECRET. The bearer POST /admin/reset on apps/hooks requires, and the bearer
# apps/web's Reset button posts through a server route of its own. Generate it
# with `openssl rand -hex 32`. Set the SAME value on cg-hooks and cg-web.
#
# A third secret, never either of the other two. ARCADE_HOOK_SIGNING_SECRET
# decides hook calls and APPROVALS_STORE_TOKEN writes approval records; this
# one can replace the live policy and empty the audit log, so a leak of any
# of the three must not do another's job.
#
# **Blank means the endpoint does not exist.** Unlike the two secrets above,
# there is no development fallback: apps/hooks answers 404 on /admin/reset,
# /health reports `reset: "disabled"`, and apps/web does not render the button
# at all. A reset endpoint reachable on a public URL without authentication is
# a denial-of-demo vector, and a published development default would be the
# same thing with extra steps.
#
# What it resets, in either mode: the four policy tables from the fixture
# compiled into the RUNNING image — which is the part a `sqlite3` session in a
# Render shell cannot promise, and the reason this is an endpoint. `demo` mode
# additionally empties grants, approval requests and the audit log. Neither
# mode touches idp.db, which holds the OAuth client Arcade is registered
# against, or loans.db, which belongs to apps/loan-app.
RESET_TOKEN=

# --- Runtime ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Render injects PORT. Locally each service falls back to its own default.
# PORT=
